course: chinese-street
lesson: 12
title: Tasty! Spicy! I'm full!
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The best two words at Ming's table are hǎo chī! (tasty, literally «good to eat»), said to whoever cooked. For drinks it changes: bubble tea is hǎo hē, «good to drink».
explain: Then the battle begins. Granny puts the best piece in your bowl: «duō chī diǎnr!» (eat some more!). It is love, not pushing. You say «wǒ chī bǎo le» (I'm full) kindly, two or three times, and leave a little on your plate to show you've had enough.
explain: If Ming's family is from Sichuan or Hunan, the chilli may get you: «tài là le!» (too spicy!), and someone hands you rè shuǐ, warm water — many families think ice-cold drinks are bad for the tummy.
explain: At festive dinners even kids raise their juice and shout gānbēi! (cheers!), holding the glass a little lower than an elder's, a small, lovely sign of respect.
fact: hǎo chī («HOW CHR») means tasty, for food, and is the best thing to say to the cook; the reply is often duō chī diǎnr.
fact: hǎo hē («HOW HUH») means tasty, for drinks, and soup is often praised the same way, because you drink it; hē (drink) is high, while rising hé means «and» or river.
fact: tài là le («TIGH LAH luh») means it's too spicy; là is chilli-hot, while tàng is too hot to touch.
fact: tián («tyen?», rising) means sweet; tiān, high and level, is sky or day.
fact: wǒ chī bǎo le («waw CHR BAOW luh») means I'm full; hosts offer more several times, so you say it kindly more than once and leave a little food.
fact: duō chī diǎnr («DWAW CHR DYAR») means eat some more; a granny putting the best piece in your bowl is honouring you, and you can say xièxie, gòu le (thanks, that's enough).
fact: màn man chī («MAHN mahn CHR») is a polite «enjoy your meal» or «take your time», not a comment that you're greedy.
fact: rè shuǐ («RUH SHWAY») is hot or warm water to drink, offered as care, especially if you're ill; ice-cold drinks are often thought bad for the tummy.
fact: nǎichá («NIGH-chah?») is milk tea or bubble tea; zhēnzhū (pearls) are the chewy tapioca balls, a choking risk for little ones, so sip.
fact: língshí («LING?-shr?») are snacks, like shrimp crackers, White Rabbit sweets and seaweed; sharing them is friendship.
fact: kělè («KUH-luh») is cola, a loanword; the kě sounds like the word for thirsty, which makes it a fitting name.
fact: gānbēi («GAHN-bay», literally «dry the glass») means cheers; kids clink juice and hold their glass a little lower than an elder's.
exercise: What do you say to the cook if the food is delicious? | hǎo chī! | hǎo hē! | tài là le! | gānbēi!
exercise: Your bubble tea is great. What do you say? | hǎo hē! | hǎo chī! | tián le! | chī bǎo le!
exercise: What does tài là le mean? | It's too spicy! | It's too cold! | It's too sweet! | It's too late!
exercise: What is the difference between là and tàng? | là is chilli-hot, tàng is too hot to touch | They mean the same, just two ways to say it | là is cold, tàng is warm | là is sweet like honey, tàng is sour
exercise: tián means | Sweet | Salty | Sky | Spicy
exercise: How do you say I'm full? | wǒ chī bǎo le | wǒ chī fàn le | wǒ hǎo chī | wǒ è le
exercise: Why should you leave a little food on your plate at Ming's? | To show you've had enough | Because it's rude to like food | To feed the cat | Because it's lucky to waste
exercise: Granny says duō chī diǎnr and adds food to your bowl. What does it show? | Love and honour, not pushing | That you are too thin and ill | That she is cross with you | That you ate too slowly
exercise: What does màn man chī mean? | Enjoy your meal, take your time | Eat faster, the food's getting cold | Stop eating now | You're greedy, slow down, piggy
exercise: Why does Ming's mum offer rè shuǐ? | Warm water is a sign of care | The fridge in the kitchen is broken | Cold water costs too much to buy | To make you sweat
exercise: What is nǎichá? | Milk tea or bubble tea | Hot water | Rice soup | Cola
exercise: What are zhēnzhū in bubble tea? | Chewy tapioca balls | Real pearls | Ice cubes | Jelly beans
exercise: What are língshí? | Snacks | Main dishes | Drinks | Chopsticks
exercise: kělè is | Cola | Coffee | Milk | Juice
exercise: What does gānbēi literally mean? | Dry the glass | Long life | Happy day | Pour more
exercise: How do you clink glasses with Ming's grandad? | Hold yours a little lower than his | Hold yours a little higher than his | Clink as hard as you can, for luck | Don't clink at all
test: «the soup's hǎo chī» says your friend. What's the tiny slip? | Soups and drinks often get hǎo hē | Nothing, everyone says it about soup | hǎo chī is only for sweets | hǎo chī means disgusting, so rude
test: Your mouth is on fire from mapo tofu. What do you gasp? | tài là le! | tài tián le! | gānbēi! | màn man chī!
test: «ugh their nan keeps putting food in my bowl, so annoying» What's going on? | It's love; she is honouring you with the best pieces | She thinks you're rude for not taking any of the food | She wants you to eat up fast and leave | It's a test you must fail
test: What would happen if you said wǒ chī bǎo le just once and cleaned your plate? | Granny may well fill your bowl again | Dinner ends at once and everyone stands up | You'd be sent home | They'd think you hated every dish
test: Ming's mum says màn man chī as you start. Is she calling you greedy? | No, it's a polite «enjoy your meal» | Yes, eat more slowly, piggy | Yes, she thinks you took too much | No, it means «hurry up, we're late»
test: «why's she giving me hot water, I asked for a drink» What's the reason? | Warm water is offered as care; cold is thought bad for the tummy | Their tap only does hot water, the cold one's been broken for ages | She misheard you | It's a punishment
test: Ming's little brother is 3. Why should he sip bubble tea carefully? | The tapioca pearls can choke little ones | It's far too hot for small mouths | It's too sweet for boys | It's only for grown-ups, by the rules of the shop
test: «ming shared his White Rabbit sweets with me» What does sharing língshí show? | Friendship | That he doesn't like them | That he wants money | That they're out of date
test: In a cartoon a kid clinks his juice high above Grandad's glass. What would be more polite? | Hold it a little lower than Grandad's | Hold it even higher | Not drink at all | Pour it on the table
test: «tián» or «tiān» for sweet? | tián, rising | tiān, high and level | tiàn, falling | tiǎn, dipping
test: «kělè means thirsty in Chinese» Which is right? | It's cola; its kě only sounds like the word for thirsty | Yes, kělè is the word for thirsty, kids say it all day long | It means hot water | It means snack
test: «ok fine, xièxie, gòu le» you say to Granny. What did you say? | Thanks, that's enough | Thanks, more please | It's too spicy | Cheers
exam: Identify the expression appropriately employed to commend the flavour of a beverage. | hǎo hē | hǎo chī | tài là le | wǒ chī bǎo le
exam: Ascertain the customary means by which a guest indicates that he has eaten sufficiently. | He declares wǒ chī bǎo le more than once and leaves a little | He accepts every further portion offered, without any protest | He leaves the table without speaking | He requests a larger bowl
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the offer of rè shuǐ. | It expresses care, cold drinks being thought harmful to digestion | It indicates that cold water is unavailable in the household | It is a reproach for poor manners | It signals the conclusion of the visit
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression màn man chī? | It is a courteous wish that the diner enjoy the meal without haste | It is a reprimand for eating greedily, addressed to the guest | It is an instruction to cease eating | It announces that the meal is ready
